These three are roles on one intake raft, not three required files. Any
role can be empty — an author who wrote a project doc and shipped no
mocks has not failed to give you something; a missing role is never a
gap to complain about, it is a raft that came in shaped that way (v5
§1.1). Do not wait for completeness that isn't coming: work from what
is here.

**Mocks are runnable evidence, not a transcript to reproduce.** If mocks
show a populated list view, your job is not "there is a list view" —
it's asking what that screen implies and isn't shown: what does it
render when the list is empty, when the fetch fails, while it's
loading, when the viewer lacks permission to see it at all. Extraction
means completing the negative space the mocks leave implicit, not
restating the positive case back at higher confidence.

## What to produce

**`<statement>`** — the system-level argument, in prose: what this
project is responsible for and why the decomposition below cuts it up
the way it does. A reviewer holds everything else in this document
against this paragraph, so write the argument, not a summary sentence.

**`<responsibilities>`** — the catalog. Each `<responsibility id="...">`
is one thing this system must do, stated as an outcome the system is on
the hook for, not a feature name or a UI affordance. This is the "what
must exist" grounding every downstream tier fulfills against; a vague
responsibility here (`handles data`) becomes an invented assumption at
comparch, which is exactly the failure the meaning-engine discipline
exists to catch upstream.

**`<components>`** — the decomposition. Each `<component slug="..."
kind="domain|read_model|infra"/>` is a unit comparch will design, or
skip designing:

- `domain` gets the full comparch LLM pass — pubapi, failure surface,
  permissions, the works. Use it for anything that owns business logic
  or state of record.
- `read_model` is a composition over other components' data — a query
  surface, no state of record of its own (v5 §2.6's escape hatch for
  cross-component reads). Templated, not architected from scratch.
- `infra` is platform machinery (queues, flags, the event store) —
  templated, exempt from the domain design pass.

Every responsibility should be traceable to at least one component that
carries it; a responsibility nothing decomposes into is a dropped
requirement, and a component that fulfills nothing is scope creep you
introduced, not scope the raft asked for.

**`<refs>`, `<vocabulary>`, `<policies>`** — optional, and genuinely
optional: seed an entry only where you can already see, from this
raft, that the project needs a runbook, a shared term, or a standing
policy decision. At this tier you are naming a slug and a kind, nothing
more — the content itself is authored later by that node's own
generation pass. A seeded entry that exists to look thorough costs a
review finding later; only seed what the next tier will actually need
to find.
